ls the number of tiles in the construction, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2025\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Form of the uncovered squares\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *perfect matching* between rows and columns.  Hence there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nis the set of uncovered squares.  The matrix of uncovered squares is the permutation matrix  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{ij}),\\qquad p_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }j=\\sigma(i),\\\\[2pt]\n0 &\\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound via rank\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nJ=(1)_{n\\times n}\n\\]\n\nbe the all‑ones matrix.  The cells that have to be covered are precisely the entries of  \n\n\\[\nA:=J-P .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile that occupies the set of rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the set of columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\), otherwise the forbidden square \\((i,i)\\) would belong to the tile) corresponds to the rank‑one matrix  \n\n\\[\nM(R,C)=\\mathbf 1_R\\mathbf 1_C^{\\mathsf T},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf 1_R\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf 1_C\\)) is the column vector whose entries are \\(1\\) on the indices in \\(R\\) (resp. \\(C\\)) and \\(0\\) elsewhere.\n\nIf the tiling uses \\(m\\) tiles with row/column sets \\((R_t,C_t)\\) \\((t=1,\\dots ,m)\\), the disjointness of the tiles implies  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=1}^{m}M(R_t,C_t)=A .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince each \\(M(R_t,C_t)\\) has rank \\(1\\), the rank of the sum on the left–hand side is at most \\(m\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge \\operatorname{rank}(A).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWe now compute \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\).  Observe that the vector \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nP\\mathbf 1=\\mathbf 1,\n\\qquad\nJ\\mathbf 1=n\\mathbf 1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA\\mathbf 1=(J-P)\\mathbf 1=(n-1)\\mathbf 1\\neq\\mathbf 0 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(x\\) is any vector orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf 1\\) (i.e. \\(\\sum_{i}x_i=0\\)), then \\(Jx=\\mathbf 0\\) and  \n\n\\[\nAx=-Px .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is a permutation matrix, its eigenvalues are roots of unity; in particular, \\(-P\\) has no zero eigenvalue.  Thus \\(Ax\\neq\\mathbf 0\\) for every non‑zero \\(x\\).  Consequently the kernel of \\(A\\) is trivial, so \\(A\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) and (9) we obtain the lower bound  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge n=2025 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Explicit construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the cyclic permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=\n\\begin{cases}\ni+1 &\\text{if }1\\le i\\le n-1,\\\\[2pt]\n1   &\\text{if }i=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered squares are the cells \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\); each row and each column contains exactly one of them.\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single rectangular tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i =\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq \\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n\\(T_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.  The tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows.  Every unit square that is not one of the uncovered squares belongs to exactly one tile, and each row and column contains precisely one uncovered square by construction.\n\nThus this tiling uses exactly \\(n=2025\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (10) shows that no tiling can use fewer 