tly **above** the diagonal, \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-1,n)\\), shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for the region above the diagonal.  \n   - Consequently any tiling must use at least \\(2(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.  \n\n4. **Conclusion.** The construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and the lower\u2011bound proof shows that fewer are impossible. Hence the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this gives  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with the \\(n\\times n\\) board whose unit squares are indexed by pairs \\((r,c)\\) with\n\\(1\\le r,c\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Normalisation\n\nThe condition \u201ceach row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 means that the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nPermuting the columns of the whole board by the same permutation \\(\\pi^{-1}\\) merely relabels the columns; it does not affect the number of tiles needed.  \nHence we may assume \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Upper bound \u2013 an explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_k=\\{(r,c)\\mid r\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;c=k\\},\n\\qquad\nH_k=\\{(r,c)\\mid r=k,\\;c\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_k\\) is a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-k)\\times1\\) situated in column \\(k\\) **below** the diagonal;  \n\\(H_k\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-k)\\) situated in row \\(k\\) **to the right** of the diagonal.\n\n*Disjointness.*  \n\\(V_k\\) occupies column \\(k\\) and rows \\(\\ge k+1\\); \\(H_k\\) occupies row \\(k\\) and columns \\(\\ge k+1\\).  \nThus no two distinct rectangles intersect, and a vertical rectangle never meets a horizontal one.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any square \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r\\neq c\\).\n\n* If \\(r>c\\) (the square lies strictly below the diagonal), then \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\(r\\ge c+1\\); consequently \\((r,c)\\in V_c\\).\n\n* If \\(r<c\\) (the square lies strictly above the diagonal), then \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\(c\\ge r+1\\); consequently \\((r,c)\\in H_r\\).\n\nHence the \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{V_k,H_k\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) cover **all** non\u2011diagonal squares, leaving exactly the diagonal squares uncovered.  \n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lower bound \u2013 at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary\n\n#### 3.1. Every rectangle stays on one side of the diagonal  \n\nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\) contains a square \\((r_1,c_1)\\) with \\(r_1>c_1\\) (below the diagonal) and a square \\((r_2,c_2)\\) with \\(r_2<c_2\\) (above the diagonal).  \nLet the row interval of \\(R\\) be \\([a,b]\\) and the column interval be \\([c,d]\\).  \nBecause \\((r_1,c_1)\\in R\\) we have \\(a\\le r_1\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le c_1\\le d\\); similarly for \\((r_2,c_2)\\).  \nSince \\(a\\le r_1\\) and \\(d\\ge c_2\\), the cell \\((a,d)\\) belongs to \\(R\\).  \nBut \\(a\\le r_1\\) and \\(d\\ge c_2\\) imply \\(a\\le d\\), i.e. \\((a,d)\\) lies on or above the diagonal.  \nBecause the diagonal squares are **uncovered**, \\(R\\) cannot contain such a cell.  \nThus a rectangle cannot contain squares on both sides of the diagonal; it must be wholly **above** or wholly **below** the diagonal.\n\n#### 3.2. Squares immediately below the diagonal force \\(n-1\\) rectangles  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe \\(n-1\\) squares that are directly under the diagonal.  \nSuppose a single rectangle \\(R\\) contains two distinct squares of \\(B\\), say \\((i+1,i)\\) and \\((j+1,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen the row interval of \\(R\\) contains \\([i+1,j+1]\\) and the column interval contains \\([i,j]\\).  \nConsequently the cell \\((i+1,j)\\) (row \\(i+1\\), column \\(j\\)) lies inside \\(R\\).  \nSince \\(i+1\\le j\\), this cell is on or **above** the diagonal, contradicting the conclusion of \u00a73.1 that a rectangle lying below the diagonal cannot contain such a cell.  \n\nHence **no rectangle can contain two different squares of \\(B\\)**; each rectangle can cover at most one element of \\(B\\).  \nBecause \\(|B|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover the whole region **below** the diagonal.\n\n#### 3.3. Squares immediately above the diagonal give another \\(n-1\\) rectangles  \n\nAn identical argument applied to the set  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe squares directly above the diagonal, shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for the region **above** the diagonal.\n\n#### 3.4. Global lower bound  \n\nAll rectangles are either above or below the diagonal, and the two families are disjoint.  \nTherefore any tiling must use at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge 2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain that the smallest possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nThus the required minimum number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
