nes, and the total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n4. **Lower bound \u2013 at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary.**  \n   *Left diagram.* In the staircase diagram \\(L\\) each row has a distinct length, so there are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows whose length is non\u2011zero.  \n   Call the rightmost cell of such a row a **corner cell**.  \n   Any axis\u2011parallel rectangle that lies completely inside \\(L\\) can contain **at most one** corner cell: if it contained two, the rectangle would have to extend past the shorter row\u2019s rightmost column, thereby covering an uncovered square, which is forbidden.  \n   Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover all corner cells of \\(L\\); consequently at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed to cover the whole left part.  \n\n   The same argument applied to the right diagram \\(R\\) (after reflecting horizontally) shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for the right part.  \n\n   Because the left and right parts are disjoint, the total number of tiles in any admissible tiling is at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction gives a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower\u2011bound argument shows that no tiling can use fewer.  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\nThus the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote by \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the permutation such that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nConsequently each column \\(j\\) also contains exactly one uncovered square, namely \\((\\pi^{-1}(j),j)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Decomposition into two Young diagrams  \n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)) consists of all covered squares that lie to the left (resp. to the right) of the uncovered square in their row.  \nIn each row \\(i\\) the cardinalities are  \n\n\\[\n|L_i|=\\ell_i:=\\pi(i)-1,\\qquad |R_i|=r_i:=n-\\pi(i).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); similarly \\(\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\nThus after re\u2011ordering the rows so that \\(\\ell_1<\\ell_2<\\dots <\\ell_n\\) we obtain the *staircase* Young diagram  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand, after a horizontal reflection, the same shape for \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nAssume the rows are ordered so that \\(\\ell_1<\\ell_2<\\dots <\\ell_n\\) (equivalently \\(\\pi(1)<\\pi(2)<\\dots <\\pi(n)\\)).  \n\n*Horizontal family.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le \\ell_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_k\\) is a rectangle of height \\(n-k+1\\) and width \\(\\ell_k\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell_{k}<\\ell_{k+1}\\), the sets \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union is exactly \\(L\\).\n\n*Vertical family.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;\\ell_k+2\\le j\\le \\ell_{k+1}+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_k\\) is a rectangle of height \\(k\\) and width \\(\\ell_{k+1}-\\ell_k\\).  \nAgain the \\(V_k\\) are disjoint and their union equals \\(R\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint from each other because every cell of \\(L\\) lies left of column \\(\\ell_k+1\\) while every cell of \\(R\\) lies right of that column.  \nConsequently the \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles cover all squares of the board except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones.  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are unavoidable  \n\nConsider the left diagram \\(L\\) (the staircase).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) let  \n\n\\[\nc_i=(i,\\ell_i)\n\\]\n\nbe the **corner cell** \u2013 the rightmost covered cell of that row.  \nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such corner cells (the row with \\(\\ell_i=0\\) has none).\n\n*Claim.* A rectangle that is completely contained in \\(L\\) can contain **at most one** corner cell.\n\n*Proof of claim.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq L\\) contains two distinct corner cells \\(c_{i}\\) and \\(c_{j}\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nBecause the rows are ordered increasingly, we have \\(\\ell_i<\\ell_j\\).  \nSince \\(Q\\) contains \\(c_j=(j,\\ell_j)\\), its rightmost column is at least \\(\\ell_j\\).  \nBut then the same column \\(\\ell_j\\) also appears in row \\(i\\) (because the rectangle extends vertically from row \\(i\\) to row \\(j\\)), and the cell \\((i,\\ell_j)\\) would belong to \\(Q\\).  \nHowever \\((i,\\ell_j)\\) lies **to the right** of the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) (recall \\(\\pi(i)=\\ell_i+1<\\ell_j+1\\)), so it is not a member of \\(L\\); contradiction. \u220e\n\nSince each rectangle can cover at most one corner cell, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all corner cells, and therefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover the whole left part \\(L\\).\n\nApplying the same argument to the right part \\(R\\) (after reflecting the board horizontally) yields that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for \\(R\\).\n\nBecause the two parts are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board must use at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Determination of the minimum  \n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction achieving exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus Matilda can accomplish the task with 4048 rectangular tiles, and no arrangement can use fewer.**"
