egin{aligned}\nR_k^{\\text{up}}   &=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\},\\\\[2mm]\nR_k^{\\text{down}}&=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n*Each \\(R_k^{\\text{up}}\\) lies entirely above the diagonal because every\nrow index \\(i\\le k\\) is smaller than every column index \\(j\\ge k+1\\).  \nSimilarly each \\(R_k^{\\text{down}}\\) lies entirely below the diagonal.*\n\nThe rectangles are pairwise disjoint:\n\n* Two distinct \u201cup\u2019\u2019 rectangles have disjoint row intervals;\n* Two distinct \u201cdown\u2019\u2019 rectangles have disjoint column intervals;\n* An \u201cup\u2019\u2019 rectangle and a \u201cdown\u2019\u2019 rectangle are separated by the diagonal,\n  hence they cannot intersect.\n\nNow we verify that they cover all non\u2011diagonal cells.\n\n*Take a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) (upper\u2011triangular).  \nLet \\(k=i\\). Then \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(j\\ge k+1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in R_k^{\\text{up}}\\).*\n\n*Take a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\) (lower\u2011triangular).  \nLet \\(k=j\\). Then \\(i\\ge k+1\\) and \\(j\\le k\\), so \\((i,j)\\in R_k^{\\text{down}}\\).*\n\nThus the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_k^{\\text{up}},R_k^{\\text{down}}\\mid\nk=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) form a tiling of the board that leaves exactly the\ndiagonal cells uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) the lower bound gives  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nThe construction above uses exactly \\(4048\\) tiles, so the bound is\ntight.  \n\nHence the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Systematic check of all smaller values**  \n\n*For \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,4047\\):*  \nthe lower\u2011bound argument shows that covering the upper\u2011triangular region\nalready forces at least \\(n-1=2024\\