\n\nA rectangular tile whose set of rows is an interval \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\nand whose set of columns is an interval \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) has\nindicator matrix  \n\n\\[\nM(R,C)=\\mathbf 1_R\\mathbf 1_C^{\\mathsf T},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf 1_R\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf 1_C\\)) is the column vector whose\n\\(i\\)-th entry equals \\(1\\) exactly when \\(i\\in R\\) (resp. \\(i\\in C\\)).\nClearly \\(\\operatorname{rank}\\bigl(M(R,C)\\bigr)=1\\).\n\nAssume a tiling uses \\(m\\) tiles with row/column intervals\n\\((R_t,C_t)\\;(t=1,\\dots ,m)\\).  Because the tiles are disjoint and together\ncover precisely the cells of \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=1}^{m} M(R_t,C_t)=A .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe rank of a sum does not exceed the sum of the ranks, hence\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{t=1}^{m}\\operatorname{rank}\\bigl(M(R_t,C_t)\\bigr)\n=m .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently any admissible tiling must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Computing the rank of \\(A\\)\n\nThe matrix \\(J\\) has eigenvalues \\(n\\) (with eigenvector \\(\\mathbf 1\\)) and\n\\(0\\) (multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  The permutation matrix \\(P\\) is orthogonal;\nits eigenvalues are the \\(n\\)-th roots of unity, and in particular\n\\(1\\) is an eigenvalue with eigenvector \\(\\mathbf 1\\).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA\\mathbf 1=(J-P)\\mathbf 1=(n-1)\\mathbf 1\\neq\\mathbf 0 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNow let \\(x\\) be any vector orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf 1\\) (i.e. \\(\\mathbf\n1^{\\mathsf T}x=0\\)).  Then \\(Jx=\\mathbf 0\\) and  \n\n\\[\nAx=-Px .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince the eigenvalues of \\(-P\\) are the negatives of the \\(n\\)-th roots of\nunity, \\(-P\\) has **no** zero eigenvalue; therefore \\(Px\\neq\\mathbf 0\\) for\nevery non\u2011zero \\(x\\) orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf 1\\).  Consequently\n\\(Ax\\neq\\mathbf 0\\) for every non\u2011zero \\(x\\).\n\nThus the only vector in the kernel of \\(A\\) is the zero vector; \\(A\\) is\ninvertible and\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n=2025 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\nCombining (7) and (10) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{m\\ge 2025}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nTherefore **any placement of rectangular tiles satisfying the condition\nthat each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square\nmust use at least\u202f2025 tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Remarks on attainability  \n\nThe argument above does **not** prove that a tiling with exactly\u202f2025\ntiles exists; it only shows that fewer tiles are impossible.  The\nconstruction suggested in a previous attempt (taking a vertical strip in\neach column) leaves many squares uncovered in each row and therefore does\nnot meet the problem\u2019s requirement.  At present I have not succeeded in\nproducing a valid tiling with precisely\u202f2025 tiles, nor in establishing a\nhigher lower bound.  Consequently the exact minimum remains undetermined;\nthe proven bound is the best rigorous result available.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem\u2019s answer is known to satisfy**  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2025,\n\\]\n\n**and the existence of a tiling with exactly\u202f2025 tiles is an open\nquestion.**"
