"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4050}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Upper\u2011/lower\u2011triangle viewpoint.**  \n   After fixing the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\), \\(n=2025\\)), every tile must lie completely **above** the diagonal (all its rows are smaller than all its columns) or completely **below** it (all its rows are larger than all its columns).  \n   Hence the problem splits into covering the strict upper\u2011triangular part and the strict lower\u2011triangular part of the \\(n\\times n\\) board by disjoint axis\u2011parallel rectangles.\n\n2. **Lower bound \\(2n-2\\).**  \n   *Upper part.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the cell \\((i,i+1)\\) lies above the diagonal and must belong to some upper rectangle.  \n   In any upper rectangle the set of rows is a contiguous interval; if a rectangle contained both rows \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\) then its columns would all be **strictly larger** than \\(i+1\\) and could not contain the column \\(i+1\\). Consequently the cell \\((i,i+1)\\) cannot be covered by a rectangle that also uses row \\(i+1\\).  \n   Therefore each index \\(i\\) (for \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) forces a **different** upper rectangle.  \n   Hence at least \\(n-1\\) upper rectangles are necessary.  \n   *Lower part.*  \n   The same argument applied to the cells \\((i+1,i)\\) (which lie below the diagonal) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) lower rectangles are needed.  \n   Consequently any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   *Upper rectangles.*  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) take the rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   U_i=\\{\\,i\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the single row \\(i\\) together with all columns strictly to its right.  \n   These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint, lie entirely above the diagonal, and together cover **all** cells with row\u202f\\<\u202fcolumn.\n\n   *Lower rectangles.*  \n   For each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) take the rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i=\\{\\,i,i+1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,i-1\\,\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. all rows strictly below row \\(i-1\\) together with the single column \\(i-1\\).  \n   These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are disjoint, lie entirely below the diagonal, and together cover **all** cells with row\u202f\\>\u202fcolumn.\n\n   The two families \\(\\{U_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) are mutually disjoint, and together they cover every off\u2011diagonal unit square exactly once.  The diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered, giving precisely one uncovered cell in each row and each column.\n\n   The total number of tiles used is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4050 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower\u2011bound argument shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum number of tiles required for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4050}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nNumber the rows and columns of the board by \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  \nChoose the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n(Any other choice of one uncovered square per row and column is obtained from this one by a simultaneous permutation of rows and columns, which does not affect the number of tiles needed.)\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\},\\qquad\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nthe strict upper\u2011 and lower\u2011triangular cells, respectively.  \nEvery tile must be a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines and whose interior does **not** contain any diagonal cell.  \nHence each tile is of one of the two types:\n\n* **Upper tile:** all its rows are smaller than all its columns (so it lies completely inside \\(U\\));\n* **Lower tile:** all its rows are larger than all its columns (so it lies completely inside \\(L\\)).\n\nThus a tiling of the board is equivalent to a partition of \\(U\\cup L\\) into disjoint axis\u2011parallel rectangles, each of which is either wholly in \\(U\\) or wholly in \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nConsider the cells \\((i,i+1)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nEach such cell belongs to \\(U\\); therefore it must be covered by some upper tile \\(R\\).\n\nWrite the row set of \\(R\\) as a contiguous interval \\([a,b]\\) (with \\(a\\le b\\)) and its column set as a contiguous interval \\([c,d]\\) (with \\(c\\le d\\)).  \nBecause \\(R\\) is an upper tile we have \\(b<c\\).\n\nIf \\(R\\) contained both rows \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\) then \\(b\\ge i+1\\), whence the condition \\(b<c\\) forces \\(c\\ge i+2\\).  Consequently column \\(i+1\\) would not belong to \\([c,d]\\), and the cell \\((i,i+1)\\) could not be covered by \\(R\\).  Hence **no upper tile can contain simultaneously rows \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\)**.\n\nTherefore each index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) forces a distinct upper tile that contains row \\(i\\).  Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) upper tiles are necessary.\n\nApplying the same reasoning to the cells \\((i+1,i)\\) (which lie in \\(L\\)) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) lower tiles are required.\n\nHence any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nDefine the following families of rectangles.\n\n*For the upper part* (\\(U\\)):\n\\[\nU_i=\\{\\,i\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\},\n\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(U_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle; its rows are \\(\\{i\\}\\) and its columns are the interval \\([i+1,n]\\).  \nBecause the single row \\(i\\) is strictly smaller than every column in the interval, \\(U_i\\subseteq U\\).\n\n*For the lower part* (\\(L\\)):\n\\[\nL_i=\\{\\,i,i+1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,i-1\\,\\},\n\\qquad i=2,3,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is an \\((n-i+1)\\times 1\\) rectangle; its rows are the interval \\([i,n]\\) and its column is \\(\\{i-1\\}\\).  \nSince every row in \\([i,n]\\) exceeds the column \\(i-1\\), we have \\(L_i\\subseteq L\\).\n\n**Disjointness.**  \nTwo distinct upper rectangles \\(U_i\\) and \\(U_{i'}\\) have different rows (they are single\u2011row rectangles), so they cannot share a cell.  \nTwo distinct lower rectangles \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_{i'}\\) have different columns, hence are disjoint.  \nAn upper rectangle uses only columns larger than its row, whereas a lower rectangle uses only columns smaller than its rows; therefore no cell can belong simultaneously to an upper and a lower rectangle.  Hence the whole family \\(\\{U_i\\}\\cup\\{L_i\\}\\) consists of pairwise disjoint rectangles.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nEvery cell \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r<c\\) (i.e. in \\(U\\)) belongs to the unique upper rectangle \\(U_r\\), because \\(c\\in\\{r+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nEvery cell \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r>c\\) (i.e. in \\(L\\)) belongs to the unique lower rectangle \\(L_{c+1}\\), because \\(r\\in\\{c+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThus the union of all rectangles equals \\(U\\cup L\\), i.e. all off\u2011diagonal squares are covered exactly once.\n\nThe diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) are not contained in any of the rectangles, so each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered unit square, as required.\n\nThe total number of rectangles is\n\n\\[\n| \\{U_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1} | + | \\{L_i\\}_{i=2}^{n} | = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nEquation\u202f(1) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles for any admissible tiling.  \nEquation\u202f(4) exhibits a concrete tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size of the problem, \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4050}\\)."
